#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""apply_batch19_step3.py - Step 3: performance and hardening.

Turns these red tests green:

  test_card_render_is_fast_enough_to_serve
  test_card_endpoint_yields_to_the_event_loop
  test_webhook_secret_uses_constant_time_comparison
  test_single_letter_search_is_not_a_category_wildcard

and brings two guards with it:

  test_category_search_still_matches_real_terms
  test_cached_backdrop_is_not_shared_between_cards

What changes:

  app/services/cards.py     Three wasted costs, measured rather than guessed:
                            the eight font faces a card needs were reopened
                            from disk every time; the gradient and its two
                            GaussianBlur(40) glows were rebuilt per request
                            although nothing in them depends on the event;
                            and optimize=True re-ran the PNG encoder looking
                            for a smaller file. 317ms -> 55ms, and the PNG
                            came out smaller anyway.

  app/routes/share.py       render_event_card is pure CPU and was called
                            straight from an async handler, so it froze the
                            whole worker for the duration - the Telegram
                            webhook and the reminder cron included - on a
                            public endpoint that needs no credentials. It now
                            runs in a threadpool.

  app/routes/telegram.py    The webhook secret was compared with !=.
                            app/routes/tasks.py already used
                            hmac.compare_digest for the same kind of secret;
                            now both do.

  app/services/events.py    A bare substring made the query "e" match
                            finance, general, health, other and travel - five
                            of nine categories - so the result list came back
                            looking unfiltered. Prefix matching from the
                            first character, substring once the term is at
                            least three characters.

The two guards exist because both fixes have an obvious way to go wrong
later. Caching the backdrop is only safe while the canvas drawn on is a
copy; without it, every card after the first would carry the previous
card's text and a share link would leak another user's event title. And a
search rule can always be made strict enough to match nothing. Both guards
were checked against the broken version first.

Usage:
    python apply_batch19_step3.py --check   # resolve everything, write nothing
    python apply_batch19_step3.py           # apply

Every file is replaced whole and guarded by the hash of its Step 2 version,
so the script refuses rather than clobbering anything you changed by hand.
Reads and writes are UTF-8 with LF, and writes go through a temp file and an
atomic replace.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import hashlib
import os
import sys
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent


def read(path: Path) -> str:
    raw = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    return raw.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n")


def write_atomic(path: Path, content: str) -> None:
    tmp = path.with_suffix(path.suffix + ".tmp")
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8", newline="\n") as handle:
        handle.write(content)
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def sha(text: str) -> str:
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()[:16]


